@meta
id: 06-16
title: Drill — semicolon down the fixtures
chapter: 06
type: drill
skills: repeat-semicolon, find-f, line-start
par: 72
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: eight clicks and eight spacebars, eight times over
open: order.fixtures.ts

@brief
Four fixture rows, two unspaced colons in each. Every colon needs a space
after it. Eight passes over the file.

The rep is `^`, `f:` `a` space `Esc`, then `;` `a` space `Esc`, then `j`.
First target described, second target repeated.

@keys
| `^` | first non-blank |
| `f:` | forward to the next colon |
| `;` | repeat that find |
| `a` | insert after the cursor |

@hints
`a` and not `i`, or the `;` will land back on the colon you just fixed.
--
Four rows a pass. Count them in your head rather than reading the file, and
check the whole file once at the end.

@solution
    j then, four times: ^ f : a  <Esc> ; a  <Esc> j

Ten keys a line for two precise edits, and only one of them named a target.
That ratio is what `;` is for: in real code the same character is usually
the anchor for every edit on the line, and describing it once is enough.

Watch what your hands do by the last pass. `^` should be firing as part of
the `j`, and `;` should feel like the same gesture as `f:` rather than a
different decision. When a repeat key stops being a decision you start
making bigger jumps on purpose, knowing the correction is one keystroke.

@start order.fixtures.ts
export const orderFixtures = [
  { id:'ord_1042', total:4999 },
  { id:'ord_1043', total:1250 },
  { id:'ord_1044', total:899 },
  { id:'ord_1045', total:15750 },
];

@expected order.fixtures.ts
export const orderFixtures = [
  { id: 'ord_1042', total: 4999 },
  { id: 'ord_1043', total: 1250 },
  { id: 'ord_1044', total: 899 },
  { id: 'ord_1045', total: 15750 },
];
